{"sounds":{"intro":"Irish spelling looks hard but follows rules. A consonant has a \"broad\" or \"slender\" sound, depending on the vowels next to it: a, o, u make it broad; e, i make it slender. The accent mark (fada) over a vowel makes it long. The sound system is different in Munster, Connacht and Ulster. The Latin line here follows the standard form with a simple, general-Irish pronunciation.","rows":[["bh / mh","like \"w\" or \"v\"","bhfuil"],["ch","like \"ch\" in Scottish \"loch\"","cheol"],["dh / gh","a rough sound at the back of the throat, or silent","dhá"],["fh","silent","fhios"],["th","like \"h\"","thú"],["ph","like \"f\"","phost"],["sh","like \"h\"","sé"],["ao","like \"ee\" or \"ay\"","gaoth"],["ái","like \"aw\" in \"paw\"","cáis"]],"notes":["Irish has no word for \"yes\" or \"no\". You repeat the verb: \"An bhfuil tú go maith?\" \"Tá\" (am), or \"Níl\" (am not).","A word's first sound often changes (lenition and eclipsis): bád, an bád, ar an mbád. The first letter after \"mo\" (my) takes a \"h\"-like change: mo cheann, mo mháthair."]},"numbers":[{"id":"0-to-10","title":"0 to 10","items":[["zero","náid","nawd"],["one","a haon","uh HAYN"],["two","a dó","uh DOH"],["three","a trí","uh TREE"],["four","a ceathair","uh KAH-hir"],["five","a cúig","uh KOO-ig"],["six","a sé","uh SHAY"],["seven","a seacht","uh SHAHKHT"],["eight","a hocht","uh HUKHT"],["nine","a naoi","uh NEE"],["ten","a deich","uh DEH"]]},{"id":"11-to-20","title":"11 to 20","items":[["eleven","a haon déag","uh HAYN jayg"],["twelve","a dó dhéag","uh DOH yayg"],["thirteen","a trí déag","uh TREE jayg"],["fourteen","a ceathair déag","uh KAH-hir jayg"],["fifteen","a cúig déag","uh KOO-ig jayg"],["sixteen","a sé déag","uh SHAY jayg"],["seventeen","a seacht déag","uh SHAHKHT jayg"],["eighteen","a hocht déag","uh HUKHT jayg"],["nineteen","a naoi déag","uh NEE jayg"],["twenty","fiche","FIH-heh"]]},{"id":"tens-and-big-numbers","title":"Tens and big numbers","items":[["twenty-one","fiche a haon","FIH-heh uh 